<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext (plain HTTP) policy for the BeOnEdge APKs.

  ONE policy for every build. There is deliberately no development exception.

  Every BOE backend the app is allowed to talk to is HTTPS:
      dev         https://dev-app.beonedge.in/api
      production  https://app.beonedge.in/api
  and `emu/boe_update.sh` refuses to build any target whose API origin is not
  https://. Nothing in a shipped build has a legitimate reason to speak cleartext,
  so the app does not carry the capability at all.

  Why no dev carve-out: the previous config permitted cleartext to 10.0.2.2,
  127.0.0.1 and localhost so an emulator could reach a local HTTP backend. That
  capability shipped inside the production APK too. If DNS is poisoned, a captive
  portal intercepts, or a tampered build is repointed, a request carrying a bearer
  token must fail rather than silently downgrade to unencrypted HTTP. A permission
  that exists only for a workflow convenience is not worth that exposure, and
  maintaining two policies is exactly the drift this repo keeps paying for.

  To test against a local backend, point the build at an HTTPS origin instead:
      BOE_API_BASE_URL=https://dev-app.beonedge.in/api ./emu/boe_update.sh (local)
  or terminate TLS locally. Do not reintroduce a cleartext domain here.

  `cleartextTrafficPermitted="false"` on base-config also governs the WebView, and
  capacitor.config.json sets `allowMixedContent: false` so an https page cannot
  pull an http subresource either. The two settings agree on purpose.
-->
<network-security-config>
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
    <trust-anchors>
      <certificates src="system" />
    </trust-anchors>
  </base-config>
</network-security-config>
